\begin_layout Standard
Suppose that for each individual result type,
 A,
 B and AB,
 we have an observation that takes one of two possible forms,
 -1 or +1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If for,
 say individual A,
 we can represent this observation by a random variable 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{A}$
\end_inset

 then we may without loss of generality write:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
E\left(Y_{A}\right) & = & P\left(Y_{A}=1\right)=\theta_{A}\\
P\left(Y_{A}=-1\right) & = & 1-\theta_{A}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

It is reasonable to call such observation a binary observation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The problem is to develop good methods of analysis for assessing any dependence of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 on explanatory variables representing,
 for example,
 groupings of the individuals or quantitative explanatory variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The usual terminology distinguishes between (a) response variables and (b) explanatory variables,
 the variables of the second type being used to explain or predict any variation in variables of the first type.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose that for two groups of individuals,
 A and B,
 of sizes 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{B}$
\end_inset

 and that on each individual a binary response is obtained.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose that we provisionally base the analysis on the assumption that all individuals respond independently with probability of success depending only on the group and equal to,
 say,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{B}$
\end_inset

 in the two groups.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 is used for a probability referring to a group,
 reserving 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 for the probability of success 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(+1\right)$
\end_inset

 for an individual.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this situation we need consider only the random numbers of successes 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{B}$
\end_inset

 in the two groups;
 in fact 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(R_{A},R_{B}\right)$
\end_inset

 form sufficient statistics for the unknown parameters 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{A}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{B}$
\end_inset

.
 It is conventional to set out the numbers of successes and failures in a 2 x 2 contingency table as in Table .
 
